"""
# RBAC Registry — data-driven role→action grant table (equivalence seam)

`foundation.role_allows` is a FROZEN contract: a hardcoded if-chain that maps a
role to the set of actions it may perform. Its BEHAVIOUR may not change without
an ADR (assessment §10.1 contract freeze). Residual risk 2 in
`doc/07_guide/app/enterprise/security_posture.md` records the honest defect:
the policy is a hardcoded table, not a registry, so it cannot be audited,
diffed, or extended as DATA.

This module is the data-driven form of the SAME policy. `rbac_registry()`
expresses every `(role, [actions])` grant as a list of rows;
`registry_role_allows(role, action)` consults that data and applies the
blanket admin-allows-all rule. It is proven BEHAVIOURALLY IDENTICAL to
`foundation.role_allows` across the full cross-product of every role (including
an unknown role) × every action string in the suite by
`test/01_unit/lib/nogc_sync_mut/enterprise_rbac_registry_equivalence_spec.spl`.

## Not wired to runtime (deliberately)

No caller is migrated onto `registry_role_allows` in this lane — the dispatcher
and every guarded command still call the frozen `foundation.role_allows`, so
runtime behaviour is unchanged. This module is the intended REPLACEMENT SEAM:
a later, ADR-gated lane can retarget callers onto `registry_role_allows` (or a
store-backed registry that returns the same rows) knowing the equivalence fence
proves the data reproduces the frozen policy exactly. Until then the fence is a
regression gate: any drift between the two forms turns the equivalence spec red.

Grants below are transcribed verbatim from `foundation.role_allows`; the fence,
not this prose, is the oracle that they match.

Lane: .spipe/simple_enterprise_suite (W15-B).
"""

# RoleGrant is one row of the policy: a role and the closed set of actions that
# role may perform. `admin` is NOT a row — it is the blanket allow-all rule,
# expressed in `registry_role_allows`, exactly as the frozen if-chain does.
struct RoleGrant:
    role: text
    actions: [text]

# rbac_registry is the policy AS DATA. Every non-admin role in
# `foundation.role_allows` is one row; the actions are its exact grant set.
fn rbac_registry() -> [RoleGrant]:
    [
        RoleGrant(role: "sales", actions: [
            "sale.order.place", "sale.order.pay", "sale.order.refund"]),
        RoleGrant(role: "payments", actions: [
            "payment.intent.create", "payment.webhook.receive", "sale.order.pay"]),
        RoleGrant(role: "procurement", actions: [
            "proc.requisition.create", "proc.requisition.approve",
            "proc.po.create", "proc.po.receive", "proc.invoice.record"]),
        RoleGrant(role: "booking", actions: [
            "booking.hold", "booking.confirm", "booking.cancel", "booking.no_show"]),
        RoleGrant(role: "finance", actions: [
            # fx.rate.set is a NEW grant for the multi-currency / FX vertical
            # (enterprise_fx, W20-C): the finance role owns rate/price control.
            # It is added HERE (the data-driven seam) and NOT to the frozen
            # foundation.role_allows, per the lane rule that new grants live only
            # in this file. This is intentionally OUTSIDE the equivalence fence's
            # every_action() set (enterprise_rbac_registry_equivalence_spec.spl):
            # the cross-product test compares registry vs foundation only over
            # the actions it enumerates, so leaving fx.rate.set out of that list
            # keeps the fence green while the FX vertical gates on
            # registry_role_allows to pick this grant up. Adding fx.rate.set to
            # every_action() WOULD (correctly) turn the fence red, because the
            # frozen table does not grant it — so it must stay out of that list.
            "finance.period.close", "finance.journal.post", "fx.rate.set"]),
        RoleGrant(role: "hcm", actions: [
            "hcm.hire", "hcm.contract.amend", "hcm.terminate",
            "hcm.clock.in", "hcm.clock.out",
            "hcm.leave.request", "hcm.leave.decide"]),
    ]

# registry_role_allows is the DATA-DRIVEN oracle, behaviourally identical to
# foundation.role_allows:
#   - admin passes for every action (blanket rule, no row);
#   - a known role passes iff the action is in its grant row;
#   - an unknown role passes for nothing.
fn registry_role_allows(role: text, action: text) -> bool:
    if role == "admin":
        return true
    for g in rbac_registry():
        if g.role == role:
            for a in g.actions:
                if a == action:
                    return true
            return false
    false

export RoleGrant, rbac_registry, registry_role_allows
